/* ============================================================================
   limit-of-one-minus-cosx-over-x2.epher -- (1 - cos x)/x^2 tends to 1/2

   The classic limit behind the derivative of cos x (Stewart, "Calculus",
   8th ed., sec. 2.6): (1 - cos x)/x^2 -> 1/2 as x -> 0. Direct numeric
   evaluation is treacherous: below x ~ 1e-7 the subtraction 1 - cos x
   cancels completely (at x = 1e-8 it prints 0). At x = 1e-2 to 1e-4 the
   ratio approaches 1/2 with a truncation error of order x^2/24.

   The double-angle identity 1 - cos x = 2 sin^2(x/2) rewrites the ratio
   as 2 sin^2(x/2)/x^2, a form that survives down to x = 1e-8 and prints
   1/2 exactly; that stable form is what any careful code should use.

   Accuracy: values print at about the 12th digit; the identity form at
   x = 1e-8 is 1/2 to machine precision.

   Run it from a terminal:
     Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/calculus/limits/limit-of-one-minus-cosx-over-x2.epher
     Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\calculus\limits\limit-of-one-minus-cosx-over-x2.epher"
     macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/calculus/limits/limit-of-one-minus-cosx-over-x2.epher
   The knob to edit is `x` below.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- the knob ---------------------------------------------------------------
const x = 1e-8

// ---- direct form at x = 1e-8: 1 - cos(x) cancels to 0 -----------------------
(1 - cos(x))/x^2

// ---- larger x: the ratio closes in on 1/2 -----------------------------------
for t in {1e-2, 1e-3, 1e-4} do (1 - cos(t))/t^2
abs((1 - cos(1e-4))/(1e-4)^2 - 1/2) < 1e-8

// ---- stable double-angle form 2 sin^2(x/2)/x^2 at x = 1e-8 ------------------
2*sin(x/2)^2/x^2

/* ---- expected output ---------------------------------------------------------
= 0.00000001
= 0
= {0.499995833347, 0.499999958326, 0.499999996961}
= true
= 0.5
*/
